<svelte:head>
	<title>Foreign Types - Macroforge Documentation</title>
	<meta name="description" content="Teach Macroforge how to encode types you don't own, such as library value types." />
</svelte:head>

# Foreign Types

A foreign type is one you don't own and can't annotate: `DateTime` from `effect`, `Decimal` from a math library, and so on. Instead of adding `encodeWith` to every field that uses it, configure it once in `macroforge.config.ts` and every derive picks it up.

```typescript
// macroforge.config.ts
import { DateTime } from "effect";

export default {
  foreignTypes: {
    "DateTime.Utc": {
      from: ["effect"],
      encode: (v) => DateTime.formatIso(v),
      decode: (raw) => DateTime.unsafeFromDate(new Date(raw)),
      default: () => DateTime.unsafeNow(),
      hasShape: (v) => typeof v === "string"
    }
  }
};
```

Now any field typed `DateTime.Utc` encodes through your handlers.

## Options

| Key | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `from` | Module paths this type may be imported from |
| `aliases` | Additional `{ name, from }` pairs naming the same type |

The rest are handlers, one for each function a built-in derive generates for a type of your own and calls on a field of it:

| Handler | Derive | Signature |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `encode` | `Encode` | `(value: T) => unknown`, the JSON-safe form |
| `decode` | `Decode` | `(raw: unknown) => T`, throwing on what it cannot read |
| `default` | `Default` | `() => T` |
| `hasShape` | `Decode` | `(raw: unknown) => boolean`: whether raw input could be this type |
| `is` | `Encode` | `(value: unknown) => value is T`: whether a value is this type |
| `clone` | `Clone` | `(value: T) => T` |
| `toString` | `Debug` | `(value: T) => string` |
| `equals` | `PartialEq` | `(a: T, b: T) => boolean` |
| `hashCode` | `Hash` | `(value: T) => number` |
| `partialCompare` | `PartialOrd` | `(a: T, b: T) => number \| null` |
| `compare` | `Ord` | `(a: T, b: T) => number` |

A derive that needs a handler the type does not configure fails to expand, naming the handler. A field is never copied, compared, hashed or printed by its structure in place of the handler.

## In a union with your own types

A field can hold a foreign type or one of your own, as `RecordId | Author` does, often through a generic alias such as `type Ref<T> = /** @default */ RecordId | T` declared in another module. Decoding asks `hasShape` of the raw input, which may be the wire form; encoding asks `is` of the live value:

```typescript
hasShape: (v): v is RecordId | string => v instanceof RecordId || typeof v === "string",
is: (v): v is RecordId => v instanceof RecordId
```

What they accept goes through the foreign handlers; everything else goes through your type's own encoder and decoder.

## Import-source validation

`from` exists to disambiguate types that share a name across packages. A `Decimal` from `decimal.js` and one from your own module are different types that happen to collide.

When a type's name matches a foreign-type entry but its **import source doesn't**, the match falls through to generic handling **silently**: the type may be a different one, and TypeScript reports any mismatch. If a foreign type appears not to be applying, check that the module path in `from` matches how the file actually imports it.

When the name and source match but the qualification doesn't, such as `DateTime` for `"DateTime.DateTime"`, expansion warns and names the alias that would match it.

## Namespaced names

A dotted key like `"DateTime.Utc"` is interpreted as namespace `DateTime`, type `Utc`. The namespace is derived from the key: there's no separate `namespace` option to set. To match the type under another name or path, list it in `aliases`.

## Setup

Generated code imports your handlers from `#macroforge/config`, which your manifest has to declare once:

```bash
macroforge init
```

Expansion fails with an error naming the missing entry until it is declared. [Expanded Config](/docs/endec/foreign-types/expanded-config) shows what the handlers become and why, with generated code before and after.
